dialogue_history.argument:
I hold that the key assumption in the target—that synthetic/virtual alternatives cannot be designed/used to provide comparable realism and interpretive demands—is false.

In particular, simulations and synthetic alternatives can be built (and are commercially available) with:
- realistic 3D anatomy (interactive rotation/slicing of accurate structures rather than only “idealized averages”);
- tasks that replicate the interpretive work of dissection (students must locate structures across views/layers, infer relationships, and make identifications based on what they “reveal”);
- anatomical variability by using multiple models/specimens/datasets (so students practice under changing anatomy conditions rather than a single static average).

So it’s not true that “typical classroom conditions” necessarily make simulations unable to deliver the kinds of interpretive judgments the target claims are unique to real dissection. Because alternatives can be implemented to require comparable identification/interpretation, dissection is not educationally necessary in the way the target argues.
